# The most the kernel image may weigh. Read by stage 05's size step, which
# prints what the image is made of and fails the build over this number.
#
# One key, whitespace, a number of bytes.
#
#   bzimage_max_bytes   arch/x86/boot/bzImage, which is what gets signed and
#                       what the firmware loads from the ESP, twice over (one
#                       per slot)
#
# History, because a ceiling with no history is a number somebody made up:
#
#   17,816,528   before the image carried modules or microcode
#   31,648,720   after hardware support was widened with every driver built
#                in, gzip compression, and 16.9 MB of Intel microcode
#
# About 17 MB of any image is microcode: every Intel CPU Intel still
# publishes for, and AMD's from the pinned linux-firmware. It is encrypted, so
# it does not compress, and with no initramfs it has nowhere else to live.
# Four server-only Xeon families are 6.8 MB of it. They stay because the
# README names servers as supported hardware, and a machine whose microcode
# is left out still boots and runs with whatever its firmware loaded, which
# is a security downgrade nobody would see. Dropping them is a decision about
# what hardware Kryptik supports, not a build detail.
#
# The value below is the last measured image rounded up, so the first build
# after the drivers moved to modules cannot fail on a guess. Once that build
# has printed the real size, set this to that size plus five percent.
bzimage_max_bytes 33554432
